course: dictionary
lesson: 3188
title: Encyclopedia: a book of facts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An encyclopedia is a book, a set of books, or a website that gives information about many subjects. Its entries are usually in alphabetical order, so you can find a topic quickly, just like looking up a word in a dictionary.
explain: «The internet brought video calls, online shops and encyclopedias, but also scams and false news, so checking sources is an important skill.» The sentence tells you that not everything you read is true. An encyclopedia is one source of facts, but you should check facts in more than one place.
explain: «The doctor Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, wrote a great medical encyclopedia that European universities used for hundreds of years.» Here the word means a big book of facts about medicine. The sentence says it was used for hundreds of years, so people trusted it for a very long time.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. A person who knows a lot is sometimes called a walking encyclopedia. This does not mean they are a real book. It means they know a great deal about many things.
fact: An encyclopedia is a book, a set of books, or a website with information about many subjects.
fact: Encyclopedias are usually in alphabetical order, so you can find a subject by its first letter.
fact: In the UK the word is also spelled encyclopaedia.
fact: Online encyclopedias, such as Wikipedia, are written by volunteers.
fact: Teachers remind children to check facts in more than one source.
exercise: What is an encyclopedia? | A game played with a ball | A pet that can count | *A book, a set of books or a website with information about many subjects
exercise: Encyclopedias are usually arranged in what order? | Random order, so you never know where to look | *Alphabetical order | Colour order | Order of size, biggest first
exercise: A classmate says, «He's a walking encyclopedia.» What does this mean? | He can walk very fast | *He knows a lot about many subjects | He has lost his way | He is a real book with pages
test: Why should you check facts in more than one source? | *Because not everything online is always true | Because books are always wrong | To make your homework longer | To learn to type faster
test: Which of these is an online encyclopedia written by volunteers? | A paper atlas of maps | A recipe book | A school timetable | *Wikipedia
exam: Read the sentence: «The internet brought video calls, online shops and encyclopedias, but also scams and false news, so checking sources is an important skill.» What does the sentence tell you to do? | Stop using the internet forever | Only use printed books for facts | Believe everything you read online | *Check information in more than one source
